# TypeScript Standards

## Imports

- Use `node:` prefix for Node.js built-in modules: `import { readFile } from 'node:fs'`
- Use `type` keyword for type-only imports: `import type { ChatInputCommand } from '@sapphire/framework'`
- Use path aliases for internal imports: `#lib/*`, `#utils/*`
- Group imports: external packages first, then internal aliases, then relative paths

## Formatting

- Single quotes for strings: `'hello'` not `"hello"`
- Semicolons always required at end of statements
- No trailing commas in arrays/objects
- 2-space indentation
- Template literals for string interpolation: `` `Hello ${name}` ``
- No string concatenation with `+`

## Naming

- Classes: PascalCase (`ExtendedCommand`, `EmbedBuilder`)
- Functions: camelCase (`createMutex`, `formatMs`)
- Variables: camelCase (`lockSet`, `botLatency`)
- Constants: camelCase (`defaultConfigExtension`, `invisibleCharacter`)
- Files: camelCase or PascalCase matching default export

## Types

- **NEVER add explicit return types when TypeScript can infer them** - only add types when inference fails
- Use explicit `override` keyword when overriding parent methods
- Use `type` imports for types: `import type { ColorResolvable }`
- Avoid `any`, use `unknown` with type guards when needed
- Leverage namespace types: `ChatInputCommand.Interaction<'cached'>`

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Let TypeScript infer
function formatMs(ms: number) {
  return `${ms}ms`;
}

// ❌ BAD - Unnecessary explicit return type
function formatMs(ms: number): string {
  return `${ms}ms`;
}
```

## Code Style

- Minimal comments - code should be self-documenting
- No JSDoc comments
- No useless comments like "// Import X" or "// Define function"
- Use block statements with braces even for single-line conditionals
